  "grammar": [
    {
      "title": "Pronouns and levels of respect",
      "body": "Tamil pronouns encode **respect**. For 'he/she' there are three levels:\n\n**அவன்** *avan* — he (boy, friend, someone younger)\n**அவ** *ava* (written அவள்) — she (girl, friend, someone younger)\n**அவரு** *avaru* (written அவர்) — he/she, respectful\n**அவங்க** *avanga* (written அவர்கள்) — they; also a very respectful he/she, especially for women\n**அது** *adu* — it (animals, things; also a baby)\n\nSo talking about your teacher, you say அவரு or அவங்க, never அவன்/அவ. 'We' also has two forms: **நாம** (written நாம், *we including you*) and **நாங்க** (*we, but not you*). The near-forms start with **இ-** instead of **அ-**: இவரு 'this person (here)', இவங்க, இது.",
      "examples": [
        { "native": "அவன் என் தம்பி.", "roman": "avan en tambi.", "en": "He is my younger brother." },
        { "native": "அவரு எங்க டீச்சர்.", "roman": "avaru enga ṭīcchar.", "en": "He is our teacher." },
        { "native": "அவங்க டாக்டர்.", "roman": "avanga ḍākṭar.", "en": "She is a doctor. (respectful)" },
        { "native": "நாங்க இங்கிலாந்து.", "roman": "nānga Inglāndu.", "en": "We're from England. (not including you)" },
        { "native": "நாம போலாமா?", "roman": "nāma pōlāmā?", "en": "Shall we go? (you and I)" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "'X is Y' needs no verb — but 'being somewhere' uses இரு",
      "body": "To say what or who someone **is**, just put the two nouns side by side: **நான் அன்னா** 'I (am) Anna', **இது தமிழ்** 'this (is) Tamil'. There is no word for 'am/is/are' in these sentences.\n\nTo say where someone **is**, or how they **are**, use the verb **இரு** *iru* 'to be, stay'. Its spoken present tense:\n\nநான் இருக்கேன் *irukkēn* — I am\nநீ இருக்க *irukka* — you are (informal)\nநீங்க இருக்கீங்க *irukkīnga* — you are (polite)\nஅவன் இருக்கான் *irukkān* / அவ இருக்கா *irukkā* — he / she is\nஅவரு இருக்காரு *irukkāru* / அவங்க இருக்காங்க *irukkānga* — he/she (resp.), they are\nநாங்க / நாம இருக்கோம் *irukkōm* — we are\nஅது இருக்கு *irukku* — it is; there is",
      "examples": [
        { "native": "நான் அன்னா.", "roman": "nān Annā.", "en": "I'm Anna." },
        { "native": "அவரு இந்தியர்.", "roman": "avaru indiyar.", "en": "He is Indian." },
        { "native": "நான் சென்னையில இருக்கேன்.", "roman": "nān Chennaiyila irukkēn.", "en": "I am (live) in Chennai." },
        { "native": "அவங்க வீட்டுல இருக்காங்க.", "roman": "avanga vīṭṭula irukkānga.", "en": "She is (they are) at home." },
        { "native": "எப்படி இருக்கீங்க?", "roman": "eppaḍi irukkīnga?", "en": "How are you?" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Question words and the question suffix -ஆ",
      "body": "Tamil question words mostly start with **எ-** *e-*: என்ன 'what', எங்க 'where', எப்போ 'when', எப்படி 'how', எது 'which', எந்த 'which (+ noun)', plus யாரு 'who' and ஏன் 'why'. They stay in the place where the answer would go — no moving to the front.\n\nFor **yes/no questions**, add **-ஆ** *-ā* to the end of the word you are asking about (usually the verb), with rising pitch: தெரியும் 'know' → தெரியுமா? 'do you know?'. After a consonant it attaches directly (தமிழ் → தமிழா? *tamiḻā*); after i, e or ai a y-glide appears (சரி → சரியா? *sariyā*).",
      "examples": [
        { "native": "நீங்க எங்க இருக்கீங்க?", "roman": "nīnga enga irukkīnga?", "en": "Where are you? / Where do you live?" },
        { "native": "இது என்ன?", "roman": "idu enna?", "en": "What is this?" },
        { "native": "நீங்க தமிழா?", "roman": "nīnga tamiḻā?", "en": "Are you Tamil?" },
        { "native": "உங்களுக்கு இங்கிலீஷ் தெரியுமா?", "roman": "ungaḷukku inglīsh teriyumā?", "en": "Do you know English?" },
        { "native": "ஏன் தமிழ் படிக்கிறீங்க?", "roman": "ēn tamiḻ paḍikkirīnga?", "en": "Why are you learning Tamil?" }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "dialogue": {
    "title": "On the train to Madurai",
    "setting": "Tom, a British traveller, gets talking to a young woman, Priya, on the train.",
    "lines": [
      { "speaker": "Priya", "native": "வணக்கம். நீங்க எந்த ஊரு?", "roman": "vaṇakkam. nīnga enda ūru?", "en": "Hello. Where are you from?" },
      { "speaker": "Tom", "native": "வணக்கம். நான் லண்டன்லேருந்து வர்றேன்.", "roman": "vaṇakkam. nān Laṇḍanlērundu varrēn.", "en": "Hello. I'm from London." },
      { "speaker": "Priya", "native": "ஓ! உங்களுக்குத் தமிழ் தெரியுமா?", "roman": "ō! ungaḷukkut tamiḻ teriyumā?", "en": "Oh! Do you know Tamil?" },
      { "speaker": "Tom", "native": "கொஞ்சம் கொஞ்சம் தெரியும். என் பேரு டாம். உங்க பேரு என்ன?", "roman": "konjam konjam teriyum. en pēru Ṭām. unga pēru enna?", "en": "Just a little. My name is Tom. What's your name?" },
      { "speaker": "Priya", "native": "என் பேரு பிரியா. நான் மதுரை.", "roman": "en pēru Priyā. nān Madurai.", "en": "My name is Priya. I'm from Madurai." },
      { "speaker": "Tom", "native": "நீங்க இப்போ எங்க இருக்கீங்க?", "roman": "nīnga ippō enga irukkīnga?", "en": "Where do you live now?" },
      { "speaker": "Priya", "native": "சென்னையில இருக்கேன். அவரு என் அண்ணன்.", "roman": "Chennaiyila irukkēn. avaru en aṇṇan.", "en": "I live in Chennai. He's my elder brother." },
      { "speaker": "Tom", "native": "வணக்கம்! உங்களைப் பாத்ததுல சந்தோஷம்.", "roman": "vaṇakkam! ungaḷaip pāttadula santōsham.", "en": "Hello! Nice to meet you." },
      { "speaker": "Priya", "native": "உங்க தமிழ் நல்லா இருக்கு!", "roman": "unga tamiḻ nallā irukku!", "en": "Your Tamil is good!" },
      { "speaker": "Tom", "native": "ரொம்ப நன்றி!", "roman": "romba nandri!", "en": "Thank you very much!" }
    ]
  },
  "culture": "Asking 'which town are you from?' (எந்த ஊரு?) is one of the first questions Tamils ask, since one's native place (சொந்த ஊரு) matters a lot even for city-dwellers. Many Tamils use only a given name plus an initial (the father's name), e.g. 'R. Priya'. Mixing English words into Tamil ('Tanglish') is completely normal in cities — don't be surprised to hear 'office', 'bus', 'phone' in every sentence.",
